The Problem Without conftest.py
Imagine you have two test files.

file1 -> test_login.py
import pytest

@pytest.fixture()
def browser():
    print("open browser")
    yield
    print("close browser")

def test_login(browser):
    print("login test")

file2 -> test_cart.py
def test_cart(browser):
    print("cart test")

Now what happens?
If you run pytest:
fixture 'browser' not found


Why?
Because browser fixture exists only in test_login.py.
Fixtures are local to the file they are written in.

-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x-x

The Solution → conftest.py

PyTest created a special file called:
conftest.py

Purpose:

Store shared fixtures so all test files can use them automatically

Think of it like a global fixture storage.



